{{Nihongo|'''Michifumi Aono'''|青野 道文|Aono Michifumi}}); born April 16, 1982 is a Sanese politician and lawyer currently serving as the [[Governor of Okami Metropolis]] since February 2024. A self-described democratic socialist, Aono rose to prominence as a housing rights activist and civil rights lawyer before entering politics. He is often characterized as a leading figure of the "New Left" in Okaiken, drawing comparisons to international figures of the movemeent for his focus on grassroots organizing, rent control, and public infrastructure.

==Early life and legal career==

===Upbringing and education===
Michifumi Aono was born on April 16, 1982, in the Tatara district of [[Okami]], a historically industrial area known for its dense housing and proximity to the city's old canal systems. His father, Satoshi Aono, was a lifelong employee of the [[Okaiken Post]], while his mother, Hanae, worked as a loom operator in a textile factory in nearby [[Tarumi Ward, Okami|Tarumi]]. Growing up in a household supported by public sector and manufacturing wages, Aono has often cited his parents' experiences with labor unions as the foundation of his political consciousness.

Aono excelled academically at Tatara High School, eventually securing a full merit-based scholarship to the Faculty of Law at [[Okami Imperial University]]. During his university years (2001–2005), Okami was a hotbed of civil unrest following the central government's proposal to fully privatize the [[Okaiken Railways Group]] (ORG). Aono emerged as a prominent student leader, organizing "train-ins" and blockades at [[Aoi Station|Aoi (Gakushuin-mae) Station]] to protest the potential loss of rural rail lines and the conversion of public transit into a profit-driven enterprise. Though the privatization eventually partly proceeded with the sale of its shares to Okaiken's pension fund, Aono’s involvement solidified his reputation within the Sanese "New Left."

===Legal career and activism===
After passing the national bar exam on his first attempt in 2007, Aono eschewed lucrative offers from corporate firms in Shinbashi to work for the [[Tokami Legal Aid Society]]. He specialized in tenant-landlord disputes, representing low-income residents facing "renoviction"—a practice where landlords used minor renovations as a pretext to bypass rent controls and evict long-term tenants.

Aono’s "David vs. Goliath" moment came in 2012, during the lead-up to the [[2012 Adonian Games]]. A consortium of developers, backed by the then-conservative Metropolitan government, attempted to clear several blocks of rent-stabilized housing in the [[Tama]] district to construct the "Cerulean Towers" luxury complex. Aono represented a group of 142 elderly residents, many of whom were [[Great Adonian War]] survivors. In a landmark ruling that reached the High Court, Aono successfully argued that the "right to the city" and housing stability superseded the commercial interests of the developers. The case resulted in a mandatory 40% social housing quota for all new developments in Tama.

===Okami Tenants Union===
Following the 2012 victory, Aono leveraged his national profile to found the '''Okami Tenants Union''' (OTU). Eschewing traditional hierarchical NGO structures, the OTU operated as a federation of neighborhood councils. Under Aono's leadership as General Counsel, the union grew from a small legal clinic into the largest housing advocacy group in the Metropolis, boasting over 200,000 dues-paying members by 2020. The OTU became the primary vehicle for his eventual entry into the Metropolitan Assembly, providing him with a grassroots base that operated independently of traditional party machines.

==Political career==
===Metropolitan Assembly (2016–2024)===
====Tatara 2nd District representation====
[[File:Aono in Office.png|thumb|left|200px|Aono in the prefectural assembly, 2020]]
Aono was first elected to the [[Okami Metropolitan Assembly]] in 2016, representing the Tatara 2nd district, a densely populated working-class enclave. During his two terms, he became the Assembly’s most vocal critic of "Trickle-Down Urbanism." He frequently utilized the Assembly’s "Question Time" to highlight the disparity between the record profits of Okami's [[Global 500]] companies and the stagnating wages of the service and industrial workers in his district.

Beyond housing, Aono spearheaded the "Universal Language Access" initiative in 2018, which mandated that all Metropolitan services provide support in the six most common languages of Okami’s migrant worker population. He also successfully lobbied for the "Blue Collar Health Fund," a specialized healthcare subsidy for industrial workers in the [[Tarumi]] and Tatara sectors, funded by a micro-levy on luxury commercial real estate transactions.

====The Tatarabashi TOD Project====
Aono’s most significant legislative achievement during his tenure was the '''Tatarabashi Transit-Oriented Development (TOD)''' project. While the central government and private developers proposed a standard high-rise redevelopment of the aging [[Tatarabashi Station]] area, Aono intervened with a counter-proposal that redefined TOD standards in [[Okaiken]].

Key features of the Aono-led Tatarabashi project included:
* '''Social Floor Policy:''' Unlike typical developments where the ground floor is auctioned to the highest retail bidder, Aono negotiated for 50% of the station-adjacent commercial space to be reserved for local small businesses and cooperatives at "legacy rents."
* '''Vertical Integration of Services:''' The redevelopment integrated a public library, a subsidized daycare center, and a municipal health clinic directly into the station complex, ensuring that essential services were accessible without the need for additional travel.
* '''Public Housing Capstone:''' The upper floors of the Tatarabashi complex were designated as "Permanent Social Housing" for municipal workers and teachers, a move that bypassed the usual market-rate luxury apartments that characterize rail hubs in the [[Tokami Region]].

The Tatarabashi TOD became a national blueprint for "Human-Centric Development." Aono’s ability to navigate the complex bureaucracy of the [[Okaiken Railways Group]] and the Metropolitan Planning Bureau during this project was cited as the primary evidence of his "executive readiness" during his 2024 gubernatorial bid.

====Opposition to Austerity====
Throughout the 2020 economic downturn, Aono led the "Okami Refusal" movement, where he and a coalition of assembly members successfully blocked the central government's attempt to cut the Metropolitan education budget. He famously argued that "Austerity is a choice made by those who have never had to choose between a train fare and a meal," a quote that went viral on Sanese social media and solidified his "Anti-Establishment" credentials.
===Governor of Okami===

====2024 Election and "The Purple Wave"====
In early 2024, Aono announced his candidacy for Governor as an independent, challenging the three-term conservative incumbent, [[Tamai Kouyo]]. Tamai’s campaign, heavily funded by the "Big Five" construction conglomerates, focused on further deregulation and "Global City" expansion. In contrast, Aono launched the "People's Metropolis Plan," a radical policy platform co-authored by labor economists and environmental scientists. 

The campaign was noted for its unconventional "Station-Front Dialogues," where Aono spent hours at commuter hubs like [[Otemachi Station]] and [[Motomachi Station]] engaging in long-form debates with citizens rather than delivering scripted speeches. This grassroots approach, dubbed the "Purple Wave" due to the blending of labor (red) and environmental (blue) colors, resulted in a record-breaking 72% turnout among voters under 40. Aono secured a surprise victory with 52.4% of the vote, becoming the youngest Governor in the history of the Metropolis.

====Political maneuvering and the "Tripartite Accord"====
Upon taking office, Aono faced an immediate blockade from the Metropolitan Assembly, where the conservative bloc held a slim majority and threatened to veto his inaugural budget. In a move described by political analysts as "surgical pragmatism," Aono bypassed the Assembly leadership by forming the "Tripartite Accord." He negotiated directly with the moderate wing of the Liberal Party and local ward mayors, offering to accelerate the construction of the [[Okami Metro]] [[Nagata Line]] extension—a long-stalled project popular in conservative districts—in exchange for their support on his landmark rent stabilization bill.

By framing rent control not as "anti-market," but as a "pro-business" measure to ensure workers could afford to live near their places of employment, he successfully peeled away enough moderate votes to pass the '''Metropolitan Housing Security Act of 2024'''. This act established the first city-wide rent cap in Okaiken’s modern history.

====Infrastructure and Decarbonization====
[[File:Aono Subway Tunnel.png|thumb|left|250px|Aono inspecting an under construction section of the [[Okami Metro]] [[Nagata Line]] in Kita-Tarumi ward.]]
Aono’s administration has prioritized the "Green Corridor Initiative," aiming to decarbonize Okami’s energy grid by 2035. His primary maneuver to fund this was the creation of the '''Okami Green Bond''', which allowed the Metropolitan government to bypass national treasury restrictions. By 2025, he had successfully initiated the conversion of the [[Tarumi]] industrial district into a hub for offshore wind assembly and green hydrogen research.

His expansion of the [[Okami Metro]] into the "peripheral wards" of the west has been characterized by "Transit-Oriented Socialization." Instead of allowing private developers to capture the value of new stations, the Metropolis purchased the surrounding land to build permanent, high-quality public housing, effectively creating a "buffer" against the gentrification usually associated with rail expansion.

====Conflict with the National Diet====
Aono’s governorship has placed him on a collision course with the National Diet. His refusal to lower the Metropolitan corporate tax to match national "Special Economic Zone" standards led to a public spat with Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]]. Aono famously retorted during a televised press gallery: "If the the National Diet wants to govern Okami's treasury, they should try winning an election in Tatara first."

==Relations with Parliament==
Aono's governorship has been defined by a contentious relationship with the National Diet (Parliament) of Okaiken. As the leader of Adonia’s largest urban economy, Aono has frequently clashed with the opposition [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] and the [[Progressive Party (Okaiken)|Progressive Party]] over fiscal autonomy. 

In late 2025, Aono famously refused to implement a national security directive that would have required Metropolitan police to assist in the deportation of undocumented workers from [[Fosia]] This move led to a freeze in national infrastructure subsidies, sparking a legal battle that is currently before the Supreme Court of Okaiken.

==Political positions==
Aono is a staunch advocate for "urban socialism," arguing that essential services like housing, transit, and healthcare should be decommodified. He has called for the introduction of a "Metropolitan Wealth Tax" on corporations headquartered in Okami to fund public housing projects. Unlike many traditional Sanese politicians, he is known for his informal speaking style and frequent use of social media to bypass traditional media outlets.

